
Multithreading allows multiple threads to run at the same time.
But when threads share data, running them at the same time can cause errors.
That’s why synchronization is needed after multithreading — to prevent conflicts.
--------------------------------------------------------------
Real life example:
Two threads try to withdraw money from the same bank account at the same time.
Without synchronization,
both threads might see the old balance and withdraw too much →
wrong result.
With synchronization, only one thread can access the account at a time,
 so it’s safe.

-This happens with threads too: if two threads try to use the same
 data at the same time, the computer can get confused.
------------------------------------------------------


Why Synchronization Comes (in Multithreading)
-When we have multiple threads running at the same time,
-they may access the same data or resource simultaneously.
-This can lead to problems like incorrect results, inconsistent data,
 or program crashes.
-Synchronization is used to overcome these problems.
-Avoid unexpected behavior: program doesn’t crash or give wrong output.

1.Race Condition:
-When two threads try to change the same data at the same time,
-the final result depends on which thread finishes first.
-Synchronization prevents this by allowing one thread at a time.
2.Data Inconsistency
-Shared resources can get corrupted if multiple threads access them together.
-Synchronization keeps the data consistent and correct.
3.Unexpected Behavior / Bugs:
-Without synchronization, programs may behave unpredictably.
-Synchronization ensures reliable and predictable execution.
----------------------------------------------
solution:
Synchronization:

-Synchronization addresses such issues by ensuring that only one thread
at a time can access a critical section of code or a shared resource
associated with an object.
-This exclusive access prevents race
conditions and maintains data consistency.
-Java provides two primary mechanisms for synchronization:

-Synchronized methods
-Synchronized blocks

-so this allow developers to control thread access and ensure safe execution
in this multithreaded environment

Synchronized Methods in Java :

-Declaring a method as synchronized prevents multiple threads
from accessing it simultaneously.
-Only one thread can execute a synchronized method at a time.
Makes synchronization simpler — no need for manual locks inside the method.
Every call to the method is automatically thread-safe.(👉 “Only one thread can use this method at a time.”
                                                       👉 “Other threads must wait until the method is free.”)

example:

withdraw() is synchronized, so only one thread can execute it at a time.
Prevents race conditions on the shared balance.
Even if both threads try to withdraw at the same time, Java will make them go one by one.
💡 Analogy: Only one person can use the ATM at a time, others wait.

--------------------------------------------------------------
Synchronized Block:
-A synchronized block is a part of code that only one thread can execute at a time.
-syntax:

 synchronized (object) {
     // critical section
 }
 -The object acts as a lock.
 - Only one thread can hold the lock at a time.
 -Other threads wanting the same lock must wait until it is released
 -Purpose:

  Protects shared resources from race conditions.
  More efficient than synchronized methods because
  it locks only the critical part, not the whole method.
  -Provides better performance for multithreaded programs.





-----------------------------------------------------------------
| Feature         | Synchronized Method                                                            | Synchronized Block                                                                                     |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Definition**  | The **entire method** is locked so only one thread can execute it at a time.   | Only a **specific part of the code** is locked, not the whole method.                                  |
| **Lock Object** | Uses the **object’s intrinsic lock** (the object that owns the method).        | Uses a **specific object** you provide as a lock.                                                      |
| **Flexibility** | Less flexible; locks the whole method even if only part needs synchronization. | More flexible; only **critical section** is synchronized, improving performance.                       |
| **Performance** | Slower if method is large but only part needs sync.                            | Faster because only small code is synchronized.                                                        |
| **Usage**       | Easy to implement for simple thread safety.                                    | Used when **only some code needs thread safety** or you want **different locks for different blocks**. |
//----------------------------------------------------------------

🔒 Synchronized Method vs Synchronized Block:

✅ 1. Synchronized Method:
-Entire method is locked 🔐
-Only one thread can execute the method at a time
-Lock is on the object (this) (for non-static methods)
-Easy to write, but less flexible

2. Synchronized Block
Only specific part of code is locked 🔐
Other parts of method can run simultaneously
Lock is on a specific object
More efficient and flexible
