1. Deadlocks
👉 Deadlock = “Deadlock is a situation where two or more threads are blocked forever,
each waiting for a resource held by the other.”
👉 Why?
Because each thread is waiting for a resource held by another thread

💡 Easy Real-Life Example
Imagine:
Person A has 🔑 Key1 and wants Key2
Person B has 🔑 Key2 and wants Key1

👉 Both are waiting for each other 😵
👉 No one moves = Deadlock

⚠️ Why Deadlock happens?
👉 4 main reasons (remember this for interview):
Mutual Exclusion → only one thread can use resource
Hold and Wait → thread holds one lock, waits for another
No Preemption → lock cannot be taken forcefully
Circular Wait → threads waiting in circle


✅ How to avoid Deadlock (VERY IMPORTANT):
🔹 1. Lock ordering (MOST IMPORTANT)
👉 Always acquire locks in same order
// both threads should follow same order
lock A → then B

🔹 2. Avoid nested locks
👉 Don’t lock inside another lock unnecessarily

🔹 3. Use timeout (advanced)
👉 Try lock for limited time (using tryLock())

🔹 4. Release locks properly
👉 Always release resources after use

💡 Easy memory trick
👉 Deadlock = “You wait for me, I wait for you”

example of deadLock:
Food Delivery System 🍕🚴
👉 Scenario:
Delivery Boy needs Order + Payment
Manager needs Payment + Order Confirmation
👉 Both are waiting on each other → Deadlock

class Order {
    synchronized void confirm(Payment payment) {
        System.out.println("Thread1: Order locked");

        try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (Exception e) {}

        System.out.println("Thread1: Waiting for Payment...");
        payment.process();
    }

    synchronized void process() {
        System.out.println("Order processed");
    }
}

class Payment {
    synchronized void process(Order order) {
        System.out.println("Thread2: Payment locked");

        try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (Exception e) {}

        System.out.println("Thread2: Waiting for Order...");
        order.process();
    }

    synchronized void process() {
        System.out.println("Payment processed");
    }
}

public class DeadlockFoodExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Order order = new Order();
        Payment payment = new Payment();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> order.confirm(payment));
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> payment.process(order));

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}
Thread1 → locks Order → waits for Payment
Thread2 → locks Payment → waits for Order
Both stuck forever ❌
Deadlock 😵

👉 “Deadlock is like two services waiting for each other’s
response in a circular dependency, causing the system to hang.”

“In this example, one thread locks the Order object and waits for
Payment, while another thread locks the Payment object and waits for
Order. Since both threads are waiting for each
other’s resources, neither can proceed, resulting in a deadlock.”
