🧠 What is tryLock()?

👉 tryLock() is a method from:
java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock
👉 It tries to get a lock without waiting forever
-“tryLock() is used to acquire a lock only if it is available,
otherwise it immediately returns false instead of waiting.

🔥 Why we use tryLock()
👉 Problem with synchronized:
Thread waits forever ❌ (can cause deadlock)
👉 Solution with tryLock():
Try → if available → take lock ✅
Else → skip or do something else 🚀

example:
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

class Test {
    ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    void show() {
        if (lock.tryLock()) {
            try {
                System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " got the lock");

                try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch(Exception e) {}

            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        } else {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " could not get the lock");
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Test t = new Test();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> t.show(), "Thread1");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> t.show(), "Thread2");

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}



| Feature       | synchronized    | tryLock()    |
| ------------- | --------------- | ------------ |
| Waiting       | Yes (forever) ⏳ | No ❌         |
| Deadlock risk | High ⚠️         | Less ✅       |
| Control       | Less            | More control |


🚀 Advanced version (with timeout)
lock.tryLock(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
👉 Waits for 2 seconds
👉 If still not available → returns false

💡 Real-life example
Think:
You try to enter a room 🚪
If locked:
synchronized → wait outside forever 😵
tryLock() → leave and do something else 😎

⚠️ Important rule
👉 Always release lock:
finally {
    lock.unlock();
}
“tryLock() is a method of ReentrantLock that attempts to
acquire a lock without blocking. If the lock is available,
 it acquires it; otherwise, it returns false immediately,
 helping to avoid deadlocks and improve performance.”
