 2. Locking Granularity
 =✅ It is a design idea / concept that guides how you use locks
👉 Locking granularity = size of the lock
👉 It means:
How much part of code or resource you are locking

👉 “Locking granularity refers to how much data or code is protected by a lock,
 whether it is a large portion (coarse-grained) or a small portion (fine-grained).”

 -locking granularity refers to how much of your code or
 data is protected by a lock at one time.

 t describes the size/scope of the lock:

 Coarse-grained locking → lock a large portion of code/data
 Fine-grained locking → lock a small, specific part

 1. Coarse-Grained Locking 🔒 (big lock)
 You lock everything together.

 Example:
 public synchronized void updateData() {
     // many operations
     doTask1();
     doTask2();
     doTask3();
 }
 👉 Here:

 Entire method is locked
 Only one thread can run it at a time
 ✅ Pros:
 Simple to implement
 Less risk of errors (like deadlocks)
 ❌ Cons:
 Poor performance
 Threads wait more → less concurrency

 🔹 2. Fine-Grained Locking 🔓 (small locks)
 You lock only the necessary parts.
 public void updateData() {
     doTask1(); // no lock

     synchronized(this) {
         doTask2(); // only this part is locked
     }

     doTask3(); // no lock
 }
👉 Here:
Only critical section is locked
Multiple threads can run other parts simultaneously
✅ Pros:
Better performance
More parallel execution
❌ Cons:
More complex
Higher chance of bugs (deadlocks, race conditions)

Coarse-grained → Lock the entire house 🔒
Fine-grained → Lock only specific rooms 🚪
