3. Concurrent Collections
-java.util.concurrent package
Why we use them?

👉 Problem with normal collections (ArrayList, HashMap):
❌ Not thread-safe
❌ Can cause:
Data inconsistency
Exceptions (like ConcurrentModificationException

✅ Solution
👉 Use Concurrent Collections
✔ Safe for multiple threads
✔ No need to manually use synchronized
✔ Better performance 🚀

❌ Normal List
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
👉 Multiple threads → unsafe ❌

✅ Concurrent List
List<Integer> list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
👉 Multiple threads → safe ✅

🔑 Common Concurrent Collections
🟢 1. ConcurrentHashMap
👉 Thread-safe version of HashMap

🔵 2. CopyOnWriteArrayList
👉 Safe version of ArrayList

🟡 3. CopyOnWriteArraySet
👉 Safe version of Set

🟠 4. BlockingQueue
👉 Used in producer-consumer problems

🔥 Why better than synchronized?
👉 Old way:

Collections.synchronizedList(list);
👉 Problem:
Locks whole object ❌ (slow)

👉 “Concurrent collections are thread-safe collections from
the java.util.concurrent package that allow multiple threads
to access and modify data efficiently without explicit synchronization,
improving performance and avoiding concurrency issues.”

👉 “Concurrent = many threads working safely together”

