🔥5. AtomicInteger :

java.util.concurrent.atomic
thread-safe integer operations without using synchronized

👉 “AtomicInteger is a thread-safe class that allows atomic
 operations on an integer value without using locks.”
 👉 Atomic = operation happens in ONE step

 👉 “AtomicInteger is a thread-safe class that performs atomic
 operations using CAS without locking. It provides better performance
 than synchronized
  and ensures correct results in concurrent environments.”

  import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

  class VisitorCounter {

      AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

      void visit() {
          count.incrementAndGet(); // safe increment
      }
  }

  public class AtomicVisitorExample {
      public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

          VisitorCounter vc = new VisitorCounter();

          // Thread 1
          Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
              for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                  vc.visit();
              }
          });

          // Thread 2
          Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
              for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                  vc.visit();
              }
          });

          t1.start();
          t2.start();

          t1.join();
          t2.join();

          System.out.println("Total Visitors: " + vc.count);
      }
  }
✅ Output (always correct)
Total Visitors: 2000
👉 Say in interview:

Multiple threads are updating the same counter
Instead of count++, we use:
count.incrementAndGet();

This operation is atomic (safe)
So no wrong result

❌ Wrong way
int count = 0;
count++; // unsafe

✅ Correct way
AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
count.incrementAndGet(); // safe

